; io, DECLARED BY HAND — the SORTS only, for now.
;
; A module in a target is a signature Σ = (S, Ω): its sorts and the operations
; typed over them (Goguen and Burstall's institutions; OBJ declares `sort`
; beside `op`). These three are S for `io`, and they live here because a type
; has ONE OWNER — `encoding/hex` declared them until 2026-09-16, which recorded
; no dependency and let a second file declare `io.Writer` to mean something else
; (declaration-surface.md §4.2, theories.md §3.2).
;
; Ω is empty on purpose: nothing here has read a method of `io` off the host, and
; a declaration is a claim (ADR 0022). `Reader` and `Writer` are interfaces, so
; their methods are unreachable until companions are built (hex-2026-09-14 §6).

(target go
  ; INTERFACE ≤ INTERFACE. Method-set inclusion is a preorder on interfaces as
  ; well as from a concrete type to one (Cardelli's record subtyping), and
  ; `io.WriteCloser` is DEFINED as `interface { Writer; Closer }` in io.go, so it
  ; is below both by construction. The generated relation has only concrete
  ; subjects, so a value of it could not be written to until this edge was
  ; stated. Go checks it at every use: the coercion emits nothing and the host
  ; compiler refuses the file if the edge were false.
  (implements go/io.WriteCloser go/io.Writer)

  (module go/io
    (type Reader      (host "io.Reader"))
    (type Writer      (host "io.Writer"))
    (type WriteCloser (host "io.WriteCloser"))))
